Lab 17: Scale Multicluster Deployments with OpenShift GitOps
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure OpenShift GitOps (ArgoCD) repository structure for multicluster application deployment
Implement Git-based scaling strategies for applications across multiple clusters using Kustomize overlays
Configure Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) for automated scaling based on CPU and memory metrics
Monitor scaling activity across clusters using oc commands and a reporting script
Troubleshoot common GitOps sync and scaling issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes and OpenShift concepts (Deployments, Services, Namespaces)
Familiarity with Git version control system
Knowledge of YAML configuration files and Kustomize overlays
Experience with command-line interface operations
Basic knowledge of Prometheus metrics concepts (useful but not required)
Your lab environment (provided pre-configured) includes:

Two or more OpenShift clusters, referred to as hub-cluster and spoke-cluster-1 (a single cluster with two namespaces is sufficient if multiple clusters are not available)
OpenShift GitOps (ArgoCD) installed on the hub cluster
oc and git CLI tools available on the terminal
A personal Git repository you can push to (for example, a fork created under your own account)
Verify your tools before starting:

oc version --client
git --version
kustomize version 2>/dev/null || oc kustomize --help > /dev/null && echo "kustomize available via oc"
Expected result: each command prints a version string or confirms availability with no "command not found" errors.

Task 1: Build the GitOps Repository Structure and Deploy with ArgoCD
Subtask 1.1: Create the Repository and Base Manifests
Create a working directory and initialize a Git repository (replace the remote URL with your own repository if you want to push changes later):
mkdir -p ~/multicluster-scaling && cd ~/multicluster-scaling
git init
mkdir -p applications/frontend/base
mkdir -p applications/frontend/overlays/development
mkdir -p applications/frontend/overlays/production
mkdir -p clusters/spoke-1
mkdir -p clusters/spoke-2
mkdir -p scripts
Create the base Deployment manifest:
cat > applications/frontend/base/deployment.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend-app
  labels:
    app: frontend
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: frontend
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: frontend
        image: nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.25
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 100m
            memory: 128Mi
          limits:
            cpu: 500m
            memory: 512Mi
EOF
Note: this lab uses the nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged image because it runs as a non-root user on port 8080, which works without extra security context changes on OpenShift's restricted Security Context Constraints.

Create the base Service manifest:
cat > applications/frontend/base/service.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: frontend-service
  labels:
    app: frontend
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8080
    protocol: TCP
  type: ClusterIP
EOF
Create the base kustomization file:
cat > applications/frontend/base/kustomization.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization

resources:
- deployment.yaml
- service.yaml

commonLabels:
  app.kubernetes.io/name: frontend
  app.kubernetes.io/component: web
EOF
Deliverable: Run oc kustomize applications/frontend/base and confirm it renders a Deployment and a Service with no errors.

Subtask 1.2: Configure Environment-Specific Overlays
Create the development overlay (representing spoke-cluster-1):
cat > applications/frontend/overlays/development/kustomization.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization

namespace: frontend-dev

resources:
- ../../base

patchesStrategicMerge:
- replica-patch.yaml

commonLabels:
  environment: development
  cluster: spoke-1
EOF

cat > applications/frontend/overlays/development/replica-patch.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend-app
spec:
  replicas: 3
EOF
Create the production overlay (representing spoke-cluster-2):
cat > applications/frontend/overlays/production/kustomization.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization

namespace: frontend-prod

resources:
- ../../base

patchesStrategicMerge:
- replica-patch.yaml
- resource-patch.yaml

commonLabels:
  environment: production
  cluster: spoke-2
EOF

cat > applications/frontend/overlays/production/replica-patch.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend-app
spec:
  replicas: 5
EOF

cat > applications/frontend/overlays/production/resource-patch.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend-app
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: frontend
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 200m
            memory: 256Mi
          limits:
            cpu: 1000m
            memory: 1Gi
EOF
Validate both overlays render correctly:
oc kustomize applications/frontend/overlays/development
oc kustomize applications/frontend/overlays/production
Deliverable: The development overlay output shows replicas: 3 and namespace frontend-dev; the production overlay output shows replicas: 5, namespace frontend-prod, and the patched CPU/memory limits (1000m / 1Gi).

Commit the repository structure so ArgoCD has a source to sync from:
git add .
git commit -m "Initial multicluster GitOps structure for frontend app"
If you have a remote repository to push to, add it now and push; otherwise you may continue using the local repository with ArgoCD configured against a local Git server, or substitute your own reachable Git remote URL in Subtask 1.3.

Subtask 1.3: Deploy with ArgoCD Applications
Log in to the hub cluster (replace with your actual cluster API URL):
oc login --server=https://api.hub-cluster.example.com:6443
oc get pods -n openshift-gitops
Expected result: pods such as openshift-gitops-server, openshift-gitops-repo-server, and openshift-gitops-application-controller show Running.

Create the ArgoCD Application for the development environment. Replace repoURL with your actual Git remote:
cat > clusters/spoke-1/frontend-app.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: frontend-spoke-1
  namespace: openshift-gitops
  labels:
    cluster: spoke-1
    environment: development
spec:
  project: default
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/multicluster-scaling.git
    targetRevision: main
    path: applications/frontend/overlays/development
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: frontend-dev
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true
    syncOptions:
    - CreateNamespace=true
EOF
Note: destination.server uses https://kubernetes.default.svc to target the cluster ArgoCD is running on. If your lab environment provides a separate registered spoke cluster, replace this with that cluster's registered server URL as shown in oc get secrets -n openshift-gitops -l argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=cluster.

Create the ArgoCD Application for the production environment:
cat > clusters/spoke-2/frontend-app.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: frontend-spoke-2
  namespace: openshift-gitops
  labels:
    cluster: spoke-2
    environment: production
spec:
  project: default
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/multicluster-scaling.git
    targetRevision: main
    path: applications/frontend/overlays/production
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: frontend-prod
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true
    syncOptions:
    - CreateNamespace=true
EOF

git add clusters/
git commit -m "Add ArgoCD Application manifests for spoke-1 and spoke-2"
git push origin main
Apply the ArgoCD Applications to the hub cluster and verify sync:
oc apply -f clusters/spoke-1/frontend-app.yaml
oc apply -f clusters/spoke-2/frontend-app.yaml
oc get applications -n openshift-gitops
Deliverable: oc get applications -n openshift-gitops lists frontend-spoke-1 and frontend-spoke-2 with SYNC STATUS = Synced and HEALTH STATUS = Healthy (allow 1-2 minutes for the initial sync). Confirm the workloads exist:

oc get deployment frontend-app -n frontend-dev
oc get deployment frontend-app -n frontend-prod
Expected result: frontend-dev shows 3/3 ready replicas, frontend-prod shows 5/5 ready replicas.

Task 2: Implement Git-Based and Metric-Based Scaling
Subtask 2.1: Scale Applications via Git Commits
Create a scaling helper script that updates the replica patch and commits the change:
cat > scripts/scale-via-git.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

if [ $# -ne 2 ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <environment> <replicas>"
    echo "Example: $0 development 4"
    exit 1
fi

ENVIRONMENT=$1
NEW_REPLICAS=$2
PATCH_FILE="applications/frontend/overlays/${ENVIRONMENT}/replica-patch.yaml"

if [ ! -f "${PATCH_FILE}" ]; then
    echo "Error: ${PATCH_FILE} does not exist. Valid environments: development, production"
    exit 1
fi

cat > "${PATCH_FILE}" << EOL
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend-app
spec:
  replicas: ${NEW_REPLICAS}
EOL

git add "${PATCH_FILE}"
git commit -m "Scale frontend app in ${ENVIRONMENT} to ${NEW_REPLICAS} replicas"
git push origin main

echo "Scaling request committed for ${ENVIRONMENT}: ${NEW_REPLICAS} replicas."
echo "ArgoCD will reconcile the change automatically within its sync interval (default 3 minutes),"
echo "or trigger an immediate sync with: oc annotate application <app-name> -n openshift-gitops argocd.argoproj.io/refresh=hard --overwrite"

chmod +x scripts/scale-via-git.sh
EOF
chmod +x scripts/scale-via-git.sh
Run the script to scale the development environment:
./scripts/scale-via-git.sh development 4
Force an immediate ArgoCD sync and confirm the change is applied:
oc annotate application frontend-spoke-1 -n openshift-gitops argocd.argoproj.io/refresh=hard --overwrite
sleep 15
oc get deployment frontend-app -n frontend-dev
Deliverable: The deployment shows 4/4 ready replicas, and git log --oneline -1 shows the scaling commit message. This demonstrates the Git-based scaling workflow: a commit to the repository is the single source of truth, and ArgoCD reconciles the live cluster state to match it.

Subtask 2.2: Configure Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for Metric-Based Scaling
Confirm the OpenShift cluster metrics API is available (required for CPU/memory-based HPA):
oc get apiservices | grep metrics
oc top nodes
Expected result: v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io shows True under AVAILABLE, and oc top nodes returns CPU/memory usage. If this is unavailable in your lab environment, the HPA resource can still be created but will show unknown for current metrics until the metrics API is reachable.

Create the HPA for the development environment, based on standard CPU and memory resource metrics:
cat > applications/frontend/overlays/development/hpa.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: frontend-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: frontend-app
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 80
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 60
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 50
        periodSeconds: 60
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 25
        periodSeconds: 60
EOF
Add the HPA to the development kustomization (rewrite the full file rather than appending, to keep it valid and readable):
cat > applications/frontend/overlays/development/kustomization.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization

namespace: frontend-dev

resources:
- ../../base
- hpa.yaml

patchesStrategicMerge:
- replica-patch.yaml

commonLabels:
  environment: development
  cluster: spoke-1
EOF
Create the equivalent HPA and updated kustomization for production:
cat > applications/frontend/overlays/production/hpa.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: frontend-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: frontend-app
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 20
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 60
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 75
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 30
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 100
        periodSeconds: 30
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 600
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 10
        periodSeconds: 60
EOF

cat > applications/frontend/overlays/production/kustomization.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization

namespace: frontend-prod

resources:
- ../../base
- hpa.yaml

patchesStrategicMerge:
- replica-patch.yaml
- resource-patch.yaml

commonLabels:
  environment: production
  cluster: spoke-2
EOF
Note on custom metrics: this lab intentionally scopes HPA to the built-in cpu and memory resource metrics because they are available out of the box through the Kubernetes metrics API. Scaling on custom application metrics (for example, requests-per-second) requires deploying an external metrics adapter such as Prometheus Adapter or KEDA, and a ServiceMonitor or scrape configuration that actually exposes that named metric from the application. Without one of those adapters installed, an HPA referencing a custom or external metric will remain in an unknown/invalid metrics state indefinitely. If you want to extend this lab with custom-metric scaling, install KEDA (oc apply -f https://github.com/kedacore/keda/releases/latest/download/keda-2.14.0.yaml, adjusting the version as needed) and define a ScaledObject that references a metric your monitoring stack genuinely collects.

Commit and push the HPA additions, then apply and verify:
git add applications/frontend/overlays/development/hpa.yaml applications/frontend/overlays/development/kustomization.yaml
git add applications/frontend/overlays/production/hpa.yaml applications/frontend/overlays/production/kustomization.yaml
git commit -m "Add HPA for CPU and memory based autoscaling"
git push origin main

oc annotate application frontend-spoke-1 -n openshift-gitops argocd.argoproj.io/refresh=hard --overwrite
oc annotate application frontend-spoke-2 -n openshift-gitops argocd.argoproj.io/refresh=hard --overwrite
sleep 15
oc get hpa -n frontend-dev
oc get hpa -n frontend-prod
Deliverable: oc get hpa -n frontend-dev and oc get hpa -n frontend-prod each show frontend-hpa with MINPODS/MAXPODS matching the values above and a TARGETS column showing actual percentages (not unknown) once metrics are available.

Subtask 2.3: Generate Load and Observe Scaling
Deploy a lightweight load generator against the development service:
cat > load-generator.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: load-generator
  namespace: frontend-dev
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: load-generator
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: load-generator
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: load-generator
        image: busybox:1.36
        command:
        - /bin/sh
        - -c
        - "while true; do wget -q -O- http://frontend-service.frontend-dev.svc.cluster.local/ > /dev/null; done"
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 50m
            memory: 32Mi
          limits:
            cpu: 200m
            memory: 64Mi
EOF

oc apply -f load-generator.yaml
Watch the HPA respond over the next few minutes (press Ctrl+C to stop watching):
oc get hpa frontend-hpa -n frontend-dev -w
Expected result: the TARGETS column shows CPU utilization rising; once it exceeds the 70 percent threshold for the deployment's pods, REPLICAS increases beyond the baseline of 4, up to the maxReplicas of 10, subject to the 60-second stabilization window.

Clean up the load generator once you have observed a scale-up event:
oc delete -f load-generator.yaml
Deliverable: A terminal capture or note of the REPLICAS value before load (4) and the peak value observed during load (greater than 4), confirming the HPA reacted to real CPU pressure.

Task 3: Monitor Scaling Activity Across Environments
Subtask 3.1: Build a Scaling Status Report Script
Create a monitoring script that reports deployment, HPA, and pod status for both environments:
cat > scripts/monitor-scaling-status.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -uo pipefail

RED='\033[0;31m'
GREEN='\033[0;32m'
YELLOW='\033[1;33m'
BLUE='\033[0;34m'
NC='\033[0m'

print_environment_status() {
    local namespace=$1
    local label=$2

    echo -e "${BLUE}=== ${label} (namespace: ${namespace}) ===${NC}"

    echo -e "${YELLOW}Deployment:${NC}"
    oc get deployment frontend-app -n "${namespace}" 2>/dev/null || echo "  deployment not found"

    echo -e "${YELLOW}HPA:${NC}"
    oc get hpa frontend-hpa -n "${namespace}" 2>/dev/null || echo "  HPA not found"

    echo -e "${YELLOW}Pods:${NC}"
    oc get pods -n "${namespace}" -l app=frontend 2>/dev/null || echo "  no pods found"

    echo -e "${YELLOW}Resource usage:${NC}"
    oc top pods -n "${namespace}" -l app=frontend 2>/dev/null || echo "  metrics not available"

    echo ""
}

generate_report() {
    local report_file="scaling-status-report-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).txt"

    {
        echo "Multicluster Scaling Status Report"
        echo "Generated: $(date)"
        echo "===================================="
        echo ""
        print_environment_status "frontend-dev" "Development (spoke-1)"
        print_environment_status "frontend-prod" "Production (spoke-2)"
        echo "Notes:"
        echo "------"
        echo "1. Compare replica counts against HPA minReplicas/maxReplicas bounds."
        echo "2. Investigate any HPA showing <unknown> targets; this usually means"
        echo "   the metrics API or resource requests are missing."
        echo "3. Review ArgoCD sync status with: oc get applications -n openshift-gitops"
    } > "${report_file}"

    echo -e "${GREEN}Report saved to: ${report_file}${NC}"
}

case "${1:-}" in
    status)
        print_environment_status "frontend-dev" "Development (spoke-1)"
        print_environment_status "frontend-prod" "Production (spoke-2)"
        ;;
    report)
        generate_report
        ;;
    watch)
        while true; do
            clear
            echo -e "${GREEN}=== Multicluster Scaling Monitor ($(date)) ===${NC}"
            print_environment_status "frontend-dev" "Development (spoke-1)"
            print_environment_status "frontend-prod" "Production (spoke-2)"
            echo -e "${BLUE}Refreshing in 30 seconds... (Ctrl+C to stop)${NC}"
            sleep 30
        done
        ;;
    *)
        echo "Usage: $0 {status|report|watch}"
        echo ""
        echo "  status  Print a one-time snapshot of both environments"
        echo "  report  Save a timestamped status report to a text file"
        echo "  watch   Continuously refresh the status every 30 seconds"
        exit 1
        ;;
esac
EOF
chmod +x scripts/monitor-scaling-status.sh
Run a one-time status snapshot:
./scripts/monitor-scaling-status.sh status
Expected result: the script prints deployment, HPA, pod, and resource usage sections for both frontend-dev and frontend-prod without shell errors (missing metrics simply print the fallback message rather than crashing the script).

Generate a saved report and inspect its contents:
./scripts/monitor-scaling-status.sh report
ls scaling-status-report-*.txt
cat scaling-status-report-*.txt
Deliverable: A scaling-status-report-<timestamp>.txt file exists in the working directory and contains deployment, HPA, and pod sections for both environments plus the three recommendation notes.

Commit the monitoring script to the repository so it is versioned alongside the application manifests:
git add scripts/monitor-scaling-status.sh
git commit -m "Add multicluster scaling status monitoring script"
git push origin main
Expected Outcomes
A Git repository containing a base/overlay Kustomize structure that drives two independently-scaled environments through ArgoCD
Working HPA resources in both environments that scale pods based on real CPU and memory utilization, observed scaling up under synthetic load and back down after load stops
A reusable status-reporting script that gives a consistent snapshot of deployment, HPA, and pod health across environments
Troubleshooting
ArgoCD Application stuck in OutOfSync or Unknown: Check oc describe application <name> -n openshift-gitops for the reported error. Common causes are an unreachable repoURL (verify the repository is public or credentials are configured) or an invalid targetRevision branch name.
HPA shows <unknown> under TARGETS: Run oc describe hpa frontend-hpa -n <namespace> and check the Events section. This typically means the metrics API is not yet available, or the target Deployment's containers are missing resources.requests, which the HPA needs to calculate utilization percentages.
Conclusion
In this lab, you built a working multicluster GitOps workflow using OpenShift GitOps: a Kustomize base and overlay structure defined the frontend application once and customized it per environment, ArgoCD Applications reconciled those manifests into development and production namespaces, and Horizontal Pod Autoscalers reacted to real CPU and memory pressure generated by a load-testing pod. You also practiced Git-based scaling, where replica changes are made as version-controlled commits rather than direct cluster edits, and built a status-reporting script to observe scaling behavior consistently across environments. The key takeaway is that GitOps and autoscaling are complementary: Git remains the source of truth for baseline configuration and intentional scaling decisions, while the HPA handles short-term reactive scaling within the bounds that Git-managed manifests define. To continue building on this foundation, explore registering true multi-cluster ArgoCD destinations with RHACM, and investigate installing an external metrics provider such as Prometheus Adapter or KEDA to extend autoscaling beyond CPU and memory to application-specific metrics.
